# Copy to .env for local work. .env itself is gitignored.
#
# Everything the application reads from its environment is listed here. Only
# the first one is safe to write down; the rest are secrets that live in the
# Vercel project (Settings → Environment Variables) and cannot be read back
# out of it. If you need one and do not have it, it has to be regenerated at
# its source — there is no copy of it in this repository, deliberately.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VITE_FIRESTORE_DB — which Firestore database this build talks to
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave it UNSET for production. The code falls back to the shop's real books
# (ibellaccessory) on purpose: an environment that forgets its
# configuration should land on its own data rather than on a stranger's.
#
#   production (main)                  → unset
#   test deployment / local testing    → ibellaccessory
#
# Both databases live in the same Firebase project, so the logins and the
# security rules are the same. Only the data differs.
#
# Read at BUILD time by both halves of the app — the browser bundle through
# import.meta.env, and the server through the same value inlined at build.
# One variable, deliberately: two names would be two things to set, and
# setting only one would put the browser and the server in different
# databases, which fails silently because each half works perfectly alone.
#
# Whenever this is anything but the production database, an amber strip runs
# across the top of every screen naming the database. No strip means the
# build is on the shop's real books.
#
# VITE_FIRESTORE_DB=ibellaccessory


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY — the Admin SDK key (SECRET)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The full JSON from Firebase Console → Project Settings → Service Accounts →
# Generate new private key, as a single-line string. Gives complete,
# rules-bypassing access to the project, so it never goes in a file that git
# can see.
#
# Needed by: creating and managing team users, sending WhatsApp, server-side
# PDF rendering. Everything else in the app works without it, and the three
# that need it fail with a message saying so rather than misbehaving quietly.
#
# SAFE to reuse on the test deployment. It is the same Firebase project, and
# because the database above is fixed at build time, the Admin SDK on a test
# build writes to the test database, not to the shop's books.
#
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY={"type":"service_account", ...}


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHATSAPP_SERVICE_URL / WHATSAPP_SERVICE_API_KEY — sending messages (SECRET)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DO NOT set these on a test deployment.
#
# There is no test mode at the other end: a message sent from the test site
# reaches a real customer's phone, and cannot be recalled. Everything else
# about a test deployment is reversible, and this is the one thing that is
# not — which is why it is worth leaving broken there on purpose.
#
# Unset, the app says "WhatsApp service isn't configured yet" and stops. That
# is the correct behaviour for a test site.
#
# WHATSAPP_SERVICE_URL=
# WHATSAPP_SERVICE_API_KEY=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CHROME_PATH — optional, local only
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where to find a Chrome binary for server-side PDF rendering when developing
# locally. On Vercel the platform supplies its own and this is ignored.
#
# CHROME_PATH=
